{
  "id": "ep074",
  "duration": 59.595,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 3.995,
      "text": "Dead stars are supposed to destroy worlds, not build new ones.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of the WDJ0914+1914 system.tif",
        "credit": "ESO/M. Kornmesser",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_the_WDJ0914%2B1914_system.tif"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.0,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 3.995,
      "end": 11.24,
      "text": "Most astronomers believed a white dwarf, the dense remnant core of a dead star, was a planetary graveyard.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Hubble image of the Ring Nebula (Messier 57).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA, ESA, and C. Robert O\u2019Dell (Vanderbilt University)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hubble_image_of_the_Ring_Nebula_(Messier_57).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.0,
          "end": 6.967,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 6.967,
          "end": 11.233,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 11.24,
      "end": 22.385,
      "text": "But in 1999, the Hubble Space Telescope spotted one hundred unidentified chemical signatures around the white dwarf HS 0209+0832.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Hubble Space Telescope sporting new solar arrays during SM3B (s109e5705).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/ESA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hubble_Space_Telescope_sporting_new_solar_arrays_during_SM3B_(s109e5705).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 11.233,
          "end": 22.4,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 22.385,
      "end": 30.33,
      "text": "Recently, researcher Jamie Williams used an updated database to solve this twenty-five-year-old cosmic cold case.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Flickr via Openverse",
        "title": "Warwick Digital Laboratory",
        "credit": "Daniel I Scully",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://www.flickr.com/photos/64192957@N04/7400981134"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 22.4,
          "end": 30.333,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 30.33,
      "end": 36.875,
      "text": "The mystery signatures turned out to be niobium, a heavy metal forged only during stellar death throes.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Element Origins.jpg",
        "credit": "JENNIFER JOHNSON",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Element_Origins.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 30.333,
          "end": 36.867,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 36.875,
      "end": 45.9,
      "text": "NASA's TESS telescope then detected a Jupiter-sized candidate planet orbiting the dead star every 4.4 days.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite artist concept (transparent background).png",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Transiting_Exoplanet_Survey_Satellite_artist_concept_(transparent_background).png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 36.867,
          "end": 45.9,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 45.9,
      "end": 54.32,
      "text": "The white dwarf's intense radiation is stripping this second-generation planet's atmosphere, raining those heavy metals back down.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "European astronomers observe first evaporating planet (artist's impression) (heic0303b).tiff",
        "credit": "European Space Agency, Alfred Vidal-Madjar (Institut d'Astrophysique de Paris, CNRS, France) and NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:European_astronomers_observe_first_evaporating_planet_(artist%27s_impression)_(heic0303b).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 45.9,
          "end": 54.333,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 54.32,
      "end": 59.245,
      "text": "This cosmic recycling shows why dead stars are actually building new ones.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of the WDJ0914+1914 system.tif",
        "credit": "ESO/M. Kornmesser",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_the_WDJ0914%2B1914_system.tif"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 54.333,
          "end": 59.6,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
